ment, connection-failure diagnostics, cancellation, simulated hangs. That's **addon logic**. None of it varies by installation method or Storybook version. It's already covered by unit tests and by the workspace fixture's existing `visual` job, and that's its correct permanent home.\n\nWhat Task 8 must prove is narrower and different in kind: **the packed tarball, installed into a real non-workspace project, works.** Dev server boots with the addon loaded from `node_modules`; it registers in the testing widget; a story captures; a changed story shows a diff; approval writes the baseline; rerun passes; static build reports unavailable. That's the core loop, and it's exactly what an installation and a Storybook-version bump can break.\n\nSo:\n\n- **Split `test/acceptance/addon-suite.ts` by what it requires**, not by depth. A **core suite** that needs only \"a Storybook with at least one deterministic capturable story\" (the loop above), and a **scenario suite** that needs the elaborate fixture plumbing. This isn't smoke/full tiering for speed — it's organizing tests by their actual preconditions, which is why my earlier \"no tiering\" instruction doesn't apply. The scenario suite keeps running against `test/fixtures/project` in the existing `visual` job, unchanged.\n- **Examples stay genuinely simple** human quickstarts, as briefed. No overlay, no temp fixture copies, no second mode in `fixture-server.ts` — that machinery disappears entirely once the full suite isn't the target.\n- **Standardize the demo story IDs across all three examples** (same demo component, same story ids) so the core suite can target them directly without per-example parameterization. Include at least one story whose rendering you can deterministically change to exercise the diff/approve path.\n- Consumer cells run the core suite only.\n\nThis keeps examples honest as quickstarts, keeps the scenario coverage where it belongs, and drops the machinery. If the core/scenario split turns out to be m