            "message": "Root cause nailed down precisely — checked React's own source for where `recentlyCreatedOwnerStacks` lives:\n\n```\nnode_modules/react/cjs/react-jsx-runtime.development.js:326: ReactSharedInternals.recentlyCreatedOwnerStacks++\nnode_modules/react/cjs/react.development.js:692: recentlyCreatedOwnerStacks: 0   (only the DEV build initializes this field)\n```\n\nSo: the code that crashes is React's **development** jsx-runtime, reading a field that only React's **development** build of `react.js` ever initializes on `ReactSharedInternals`. It's `undefined` — meaning whatever `ReactSharedInternals` object this addon's bundled jsx-runtime is actually bound to was NOT initialized by that same dev build. Two React module instances, disagreeing on shape.\n\nWhy: `src/manager.tsx` uses the automatic JSX transform, so `dist/manager.js` imports `{ jsx, jsxs }` from `\"react/jsx-runtime\"` (confirmed in the built output — this is correct, standard tsdown/tsc output for `\"jsx\": \"react-jsx\"`). This addon's docs/release plan already state \"the manager consumes Storybook's bundled React\" — i.e., Storybook's manager-builder is supposed to alias bare `\"react\"`/`\"react-dom\"` imports to its own global singleton so addon manager bundles never carry their own React copy. My reading: that aliasing most likely covers `\"react\"` and `\"react-dom\"` but not the `\"react/jsx-runtime\"` subpath specifically, so esbuild (Storybook's manager-builder) inlines a *fresh* copy of React's jsx-runtime module into the addon's bundle — one that expects to share `ReactSharedInternals` with a React instance that actually matches its own dev/prod build, but ends up bound to whatever Storybook's global \"react\" instance actually is (possibly a different React build Storybook vendors for its own chrome). Consistent with everything I've found: reproduces identically regardless of pnpm/npm/workspace-link/packed-tarball — it's purely about consuming the **compiled** manager entry through **any** node_modules resolution path, which is exactly what makes `react/jsx-runtime` get bundled this way; source-mode never hits it because Storybook compiles `src/manager.tsx` itself and handles the JSX transform + aliasing consistently.\n\n**Concrete, testable fix candidate**: have tsdown emit the manager entry (and anything importing JSX that ships in the manager bundle) using the **classic** JSX transform (`React.createElement`) instead of automatic, so `dist/manager.js` never imports `\"react/jsx-runtime\"` at all — it already does `import React from \"react\"` for exactly this kind of interop. This is a known pattern for library/addon code that needs to stay compatible with a host's own React aliasing without relying on the host also aliasing the jsx-runtime subpath. Would need to verify: (a) it fixes the crash in a packed/workspace-linked consumer, (b) it doesn't regress `test/fixtures/project`'s source-mode flow (unaffected either way, since that path never touches `dist/manager.js`), (c) whether `src/manager/Panel.tsx`/`TestProviderRow.tsx` (also JSX, also compiled into the manager bundle) need the same treatment.\n\nI have a fast local iteration loop now (build → point an already-running example at the fresh dist → reload in the Chrome browser tool → read console) — I can implement and verify this myself if you want me to take it, or hand off if this belongs to whoever owns general addon maintenance rather than Task 8. Given how central this is to the `consumer` job actually going green, I lean toward fixing it now rather than shipping Task 8 with 3 permanently-red required jobs — but it's your call on scope. Let me know.",
