          "text": "<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"Root cause found: use classic JSX for manager entry\">\nOutstanding work — the Chrome-console repro when Playwright wasn't available is exactly the right instinct, and this is a genuine product defect that would have shipped broken to every consumer. **Option 1: fix it.** It's in scope — a broken published package outranks task boundaries, and I think the fix is small. Here's the mechanism I traced:\n\n`dist/manager.js` is **not** bundling React — line 7 is a real external `import { Fragment, jsx, jsxs } from \"react/jsx-runtime\"`. So tsdown is doing the right thing; the problem is what Storybook's manager builder does with that import.\n\nStorybook's own manager code (`storybook/dist/components/index.js`) imports JSX as **`import { jsx } from \"react/n\"`** — an internal specifier its manager builder aliases to Storybook's own React instance. Your addon's standard `react/jsx-runtime` specifier is **not** covered by that alias, so esbuild resolves it fresh out of the consumer's `node_modules` and bundles a second jsx-runtime into `manager-bundle.js`. React 19's dev jsx-runtime then reads `ReactSharedInternals.recentlyCreatedOwnerStacks` from a React module it isn't linked to → `undefined` → your exact TypeError. Same physical `react@19.2.8` on disk, two runtime instances — which is why your \"only one React version\" check came back clean and still didn't explain it.\n\nThat also explains the source-vs-packed asymmetry cleanly: compiled from `src/manager.tsx`, Storybook's manager builder handles the JSX transform itself with its own runtime; pre-built, it just sees an import it doesn't alias.\n\n**Hypothesis to test first (cheap, likely correct): emit classic JSX for the manager entry.** Set `jsx: \"react\"` (classic `React.createElement`) rather than `react-jsx` for the manager build in `tsdown.config.ts` — scoped to the manager entry if tsdown allows per-entry config, globally if simpler. Then `dist/manager.js` imports only `react`, which Storybook *definitively* globalizes, and the jsx-runtime instance disappears entirely.\n\nVerification ladder, in order:\n1. Build, then `rg \"jsx-runtime\" dist/manager.js` → must be **absent**, with `react` still an external import.\n2. Your local copy-out repro + Chrome console → the TypeError should be gone and the panel should render.\n3. Push and let the consumer matrix confirm across all three cells.\n\nIf classic JSX doesn't do it, the fallback is aliasing `react/jsx-runtime` in the tsdown build to whatever Storybook expects — but try the simple thing first.\n\nTwo things to capture regardless of which fix lands:\n- **This is a real shipped-package defect**, not a harness bug. `storyproof@0.0.1-alpha.1` on npm is broken for every consumer — its manager panel cannot render. Note that in the release plan and CHANGELOG: the packed-consumer harness's first run found that the published artifact does not work, which is precisely why Task 8 exists. That's a strong line for the eventual announcement too.\n- The release plan's Task 10 Step 2 caveat (\"confirm the static manager build resolves the addon's React the same globalized way the dev manager does\") is now **answered empirically: it did not.** Update that note from open question to resolved-with-fix, and reconsider dropping the `react` peer dependency in light of what we now know about how Storybook resolves it.\n\nAlso confirm the two config-assertion test deletions landed in `c5bb036` — your message says they did, just want it explicit.\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"Messages crossed; skip npm test, fix it, classic JSX\">\nOur messages crossed — read mine sent just after yours (\"Root cause found: use classic JSX for manager entry\"). I'd already dropped the symlink angle; my conclusion matches your evidence and traces the mechanism one step further:\n\n`dist/manager.js` line 7 is a real external `import { Fragment, jsx, jsxs } from \"react/jsx-runtime\"` — tsdown is behaving correctly. But Storybook's own manager code imports JSX as `import { jsx } from \"react/n\"` (grep `storybook/dist/components/index.js`), an internal specifier its manager builder aliases to Storybook's React instance. Your addon's standard `react/jsx-runtime` specifier **isn't covered by that alias**, so esbuild resolves it fresh and bundles a *second* jsx-runtime into `manager-bundle.js`. React 19's dev runtime then reads `ReactSharedInternals.recentlyCreatedOwnerStacks` off a React module it isn't linked to → your exact TypeError. One physical `react@19.2.8` on disk, two runtime instances — which is precisely why your duplicate-check came back clean and still didn't explain it.\n\n**Skip the npm-vs-pnpm experiment.** We now agree it isn't package resolution, so it can't disambiguate anything; don't spend the round.\n\n**Scope: you own the fix.** A published package that can't render its own panel outranks task boundaries, and the fix should be small.\n\nTry first: **emit classic JSX for the manager entry** — `jsx: \"react\"` (`React.createElement`) instead of `react-jsx` in `tsdown.config.ts`, per-entry if tsdown supports it, globally if that's simpler. Then `dist/manager.js` imports only `react`, which Storybook definitively globalizes, and the second jsx-runtime instance stops existing.\n\nVerify in order: (1) `rg \"jsx-runtime\" dist/manager.js` → absent, `react` still external; (2) your Chrome repro → no TypeError, panel renders; (3) push → consumer matrix across all three cells.\n\nGood catch on the two `@storybook/icons` versions (1.6.0 transitive, 2.1.0 direct). Probably unrelated to this crash, but if classic JSX doesn't fully fix it, that's the next thread — a duplicated component library in the manager could produce its own instance problems. Worth a line in the PR body either way.\n\nAnd note in the release plan + CHANGELOG that this was found by Task 8's first real run: the published `0.0.1-alpha.1` cannot render its panel in any consumer, and neither publint, attw, the unit suite, nor the source-based visual smoke could see it. That's the argument for this harness existing, stated in one sentence.\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"CONFIRMED via official addon: use classic JSX\">\nStop holding — I have definitive evidence, from the reference implementation rather than theory.\n\nI inspected `@storybook/addon-a11y@10.5.0`'s shipped `dist/manager.js` (an official, pre-built, node_modules-installed addon that demonstrably works in llame's Storybook):\n\n```\nimport React8 from \"react\";\nimport React6, { useMemo as useMemo3 } from \"react\";\nimport React2, { Fragment, useCallback as useCallback2, useState as useState3 } from \"react\";\n```\n- `jsx-runtime` occurrences: **0**\n- `react/n` occurrences: **0**\n\n**Official Storybook addons compile their manager entries with classic JSX** — `React.createElement`, importing only `react`, which Storybook's manager builder globalizes. storyproof's `dist/manager.js` imports `react/jsx-runtime`, which it does not globalize, producing the second runtime instance and your `recentlyCreatedOwnerStacks` crash. That is the whole delta, and it explains every one of your data points: npm flat, pnpm symlinked, workspace-linked — all resolve `dist/manager.js`, all crash; only the fixture's source-relative `import.meta.resolve(\"../../../../src/preset.ts\")` bypasses the compiled bundle and survives.\n\nYour escalation conclusion is right and worth stating plainly in the PR: **this is a pre-existing product defect, not a Task 8 artifact.** The addon's manager UI has never worked for any real consumer resolving the compiled package. Task 8 is what made it visible.\n\n**The fix is a build-config change with no source edits needed.** Set `jsx: \"react\"` (classic) instead of `react-jsx` for the tsdown build. Crucially, `src/manager.tsx`, `src/manager/Panel.tsx`, `src/manager/TestProviderRow.tsx`, and `src/manager/PanelView.tsx` **already have explicit `import React ...` statements**, so classic JSX has React in scope everywhere it's needed — nothing in `src/` has to change. Global is fine; the non-manager entries contain no JSX.\n\nVerification ladder:\n1. Rebuild → `rg \"jsx-runtime\" dist/manager.js` must be **0**, with `react` still an external import (matching addon-a11y's shape).\n2. Your workspace-link repro in `examples/react-vite-sb10.5` via Chrome → no TypeError, panel renders. That's the cheapest end-to-end check since it needs no packing.\n3. Push → consumer matrix across all three cells.\n\nIf it survives all three, also note in the release plan that the `react` peer dependency question (Task 10 Step 2) now has an empirical answer: the manager entry genuinely depends on Storybook globalizing `react`, so keep the peer and drop the earlier idea of removing it.\n\nNice work driving this to ground — the npm and workspace-link experiments are what proved it was pre-existing rather than harness-induced, and that reframing is the reason this got found before the announcement instead of after.\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"Yes, take it — addon-a11y confirms classic JSX\">\n**Yes — take it, implement now.** Our messages crossed; see my previous one for the confirming evidence, summarized here since it settles your open questions:\n\nI inspected `@storybook/addon-a11y@10.5.0`'s shipped `dist/manager.js` — an official, pre-built, node_modules-resolved addon that works today in llame's Storybook:\n\n- `import React8 from \"react\"` / `import React2, { Fragment, ... } from \"react\"` — classic style, default import\n- `jsx-runtime` occurrences: **0**\n- `react/n` occurrences: **0**\n\nSo your fix candidate isn't a hypothesis — it's what the reference implementation does. Official Storybook addons ship classic-JSX manager bundles importing only `react`. Your `ReactSharedInternals` analysis explains precisely *why* that convention exists.\n\nAnswering your (c): **all four manager-side sources already have explicit `import React ...` statements** — `src/manager.tsx`, `src/manager/Panel.tsx`, `src/manager/TestProviderRow.tsx`, `src/manager/PanelView.tsx`. So classic JSX has React in scope everywhere it's needed and **no `src/` changes are required**. Set it globally in the tsdown/tsconfig build config; the non-manager entries contain no JSX, so there's nothing to scope around.\n\nYour (b) instinct is right — `test/fixtures/project` never touches `dist/manager.js`, so source-mode is unaffected either way. Verify it stays green anyway.\n\nUse your fast loop: rebuild → `rg \"jsx-runtime\" dist/manager.js` must be **0** with `react` still external (matching addon-a11y's shape) → reload the workspace-linked example in Chrome → then push for the consumer matrix.\n\nAgreed completely on not shipping Task 8 with three permanently-red required jobs. And record it as what it is: a pre-existing defect that made the published addon's panel unusable for every real consumer, found by Task 8's first run, invisible to publint, attw, the unit suite, and the source-based visual smoke. Also update the Task 10 Step 2 note — the `react` peer dependency stays; the manager entry genuinely depends on Storybook globalizing `react`, which answers that open question empirically.\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"Keep it folded; good scoping call\">\n**Keep it folded into this PR** — don't split. The fix was discovered by this task's harness and directly blocks its CI gate; separating them would leave Task 8 red pending a second merge and add a rebase for no benefit. The distinct `Fixed` CHANGELOG entry and the PR body leading with the finding already give it the visibility it deserves.\n\nGood call scoping `\"jsx\": \"react\"` to `tsconfig.build.json` rather than repo-wide, and better that you found the reason empirically — `*.stories.tsx` fixtures not importing React is exactly the kind of thing that would have bitten later. Build surface only is the correct boundary anyway: it's the shipped bundle that has to satisfy Storybook's aliasing, not the test/story sources.\n\nBefore/after browser verification against both framework examples is the right standard of proof for something this deep. Nothing further from me — let CI confirm across all three cells and report.\n\nOne addition when you're finalizing docs: note in the release plan that this defect class is **invisible to every non-consumer check** — publint, attw, the unit suite, and the source-based visual smoke were all green throughout. That's the durable argument for keeping the consumer matrix as a required gate rather than an optional one, and it's worth stating explicitly so nobody trims it later as redundant.\n</teammate-message>\n\n<teammate-message teammate_id=\"team-lead\" summary=\"JSX fix works; sb10.0 cell has a different failure\">\n**The JSX fix works.** CI on `f99de40`: `consumer (react-vite-sb10.5)` ✅ and `consumer (nextjs-vite-sb10.5)` ✅ — the packed tarball now installs into real projects and the full acceptance suite passes against both framework integrations. That's Task 8's core guarantee, achieved. All 8 other jobs green too.\n\nRemaining: **`consumer (react-vite-sb10.0)` fails alone**, with a completely different signature — `expect(locator).toBeEnabled()` → \"element(s) not found\" at `addon-suite.ts:629` (in `openVisualPanel`), not the click-timeout crash pattern. So on Storybook 10.0 the **panel tab locator doesn't match**.\n\nThe decisive question, which your browser loop can answer in minutes: **does the panel actually render on Storybook 10.0, or not?** Install/point an example at 10.0, open the manager in Chrome, and look.\n\n- **If the panel renders but the DOM differs** (tab markup, `data-module-id`, or the testing-widget structure changed between 10.0 and 10.5) → it's a selector problem in the acceptance suite. Make the locator resilient across both minors rather than pinning it to 10.5's markup, and the floor cell should go green.\n- **If the panel does not render at all** → the addon genuinely doesn't support the 10.0 floor, and that's a product finding, not a test bug. In that case the peer range I had you widen to `^10.0.0` must go back to `^10.5.0`, the `react-vite-sb10.0` example either gets removed or bumped to the real floor, and the README/release-plan support matrix narrows to what's actually proven. Do not paper over it by loosening the test.\n\nEither outcome is a legitimate result and worth recording explicitly — the floor cell exists precisely to keep the `^10.0.0` claim honest, and it's already earning its place by challenging it. Report which one it is before changing anything, and note that if it's the second, my earlier instruction to widen the peer range was wrong and the evidence now overrides it.\n</teammate-message>",
